WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.917
LangGraph.

2
00:00:01.197 --> 00:00:09.112
LangGraph, from LangChain, calls itself a low-level orchestration framework and runtime for long-running, stateful agents.

3
00:00:09.392 --> 00:00:22.704
You describe the agent as a graph: a shared state object, nodes (plain functions or model calls that read state and return updates), and edges that decide which node runs next, including conditional edges.

4
00:00:22.984 --> 00:00:28.765
It does not prescribe prompts or an agent architecture, and it does not require LangChain itself.

5
00:00:29.045 --> 00:00:34.933
Its strengths are the things that are tedious to build by hand: Persistence and durable execution.

6
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:39.907
Compile the graph with a checkpointer and every step is saved under a thread_id.

7
00:00:40.187 --> 00:00:42.917
A run can recover after a failure and resume.

8
00:00:43.197 --> 00:00:52.605
The docs point out that the in-memory checkpointer loses everything on restart, and recommend database-backed ones (Postgres, SQLite) for production.

9
00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:53.931
Human-in-the-loop.

10
00:00:54.211 --> 00:01:00.888
Calling interrupt() inside a node pauses the graph, saves state, and surfaces a value to your application.

11
00:01:01.168 --> 00:01:05.029
You resume later by invoking the graph with Command(resume=.)

12
00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:06.440
on the same thread.

13
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.477
Observability and deployment through LangSmith, a separate product, if you want it.

14
00:01:11.757 --> 00:01:24.451
One documented caveat deserves attention because it is a classic agent bug: on resume, the interrupted node runs again from its beginning, so any code before the interrupt() call executes twice.

15
00:01:24.731 --> 00:01:28.741
The docs advise making side effects before an interrupt idempotent.

16
00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:34.845
That is the same idempotency rule from Studio 1, now enforced by the framework’s semantics.

17
00:01:35.125 --> 00:01:36.896
The cost is conceptual weight.

18
00:01:37.176 --> 00:01:42.467
For a single loop with a few tools, a graph adds vocabulary without adding capability.

19
00:01:42.747 --> 00:01:51.856
LangGraph pays off when the control flow is genuinely a graph, when runs must survive restarts, or when people must approve steps that may take hours.
